  "content": {
    "id": "p23-q5-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "5",
    "prompt": "The height of the tide in a small beach town is measured along a sea wall. The water levels oscillate between 2 metres at low tide and 5 metres at high tide. On a particular day, low tide occurred at 6 am and high tide occurred at noon. Every 12 hours, the cycle repeats. Let $t$ be the time in hours after 6 am. Find an equation to model the height $H$ of water, in metres, at time $t$.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$H(t)=3.5-1.5\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi t}{6}\\right)$, where $H$ is in metres and $t$ is in hours.",
      "worked": "The mean height is $\\dfrac{5+2}{2}=3.5$ metres and the amplitude is $\\dfrac{5-2}{2}=1.5$ metres. The period is 12 hours, so\n$$\\begin{align*}n&=\\frac{2\\pi}{12}\\\\n&=\\frac{\\pi}{6}.\\end{align*}$$\nThe height starts at its minimum, so use a negative cosine term:\n$$H(t)=3.5-1.5\\cos\\left(\\frac{\\pi t}{6}\\right).$$\nCheck: $H(0)=2$ metres at 6 am and $H(6)=5$ metres at noon.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 42
  },
